==Uses==
===Insect repellent===
American beautyberry has been used as a folk remedy to prevent mosquito bites.<ref name=ARS/><ref>[https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2006/07/060703091932.htm Scientists Confirm Folk Remedy Repels Mosquitoes] [[University of Mississippi]] (ScienceDaily) July 3, 2006</ref>  Four chemicals isolated from ''Callicarpa'' have been shown to act as insect repellents:[[borneol]],<ref>{{cite web
 |url          = http://sun.ars-grin.gov:8080/npgspub/xsql/duke/plantdisp.xsql?taxon=2372
 |title        = Species Information
 |publisher    = sun.ars-grin.gov
 |access-date   = 2008-03-02
 |archive-url  = https://web.archive.org/web/20041110095600/http://sun.ars-grin.gov:8080/npgspub/xsql/duke/plantdisp.xsql?taxon=2372
 |archive-date = 2004-11-10
 |url-status     = dead
}}</ref> [[callicarpenal]], [[intermedeol]], and [[spathulenol]].<ref name=Cantrell>{{cite journal | doi = 10.1021/jf0509308 | title = Isolation and Identification of Mosquito Bite Deterrent Terpenoids from Leaves of American (Callicarpa americana) and Japanese (Callicarpa japonica) Beautyberry | year = 2005 | last1 = Cantrell | first1 = C. L. | last2 = Klun | first2 = J. A. | last3 = Bryson | first3 = C. T. | last4 = Kobaisy | first4 = M. | last5 = Duke | first5 = S. O. | journal = Journal of Agricultural and Food Chemistry | volume = 53 | issue = 15 | pages = 5948–53 | pmid = 16028979}}</ref>   The use of callicarpenal has been patented by the [[United States Department of Agriculture]]'s [[Agricultural Research Service]] as a mosquito repellent.<ref name=ARS>{{cite journal | publisher = [[Agricultural Research Service]] | date = February 6, 2006 | journal = Agricultural Research | title =   Learning from our elders: Folk Remedy Yields Mosquito-Thwarting Compound | url = https://agresearchmag.ars.usda.gov/2006/feb/mosquito}}</ref>

